    "wall_seconds": 2.899761562992353,
    "content": "\n\nTo a compiler engineer, speculative decoding is best understood not as a probabilistic guessing game, but as a **speculative execution pipeline** applied to the autoregressive generation of tokens. It is an optimization technique designed to mitigate the memory-bandwidth bottleneck inherent in Large Language Model (LLM) inference.\n\nHere is the breakdown of the mechanism, verification, and acceptance logic.\n\n### 1. The Problem: Memory-Bandwidth Bound\nIn standard autoregressive decoding, generating the $n$-th token requires a full forward pass through the model.\n*   **Compute:** The matrix multiplications (GEMMs) are heavy.\n*   **Memory:** The model weights (tens of GBs) must be loaded from HBM (High Bandwidth Memory) to the GPU for *every single token*.\n*   **Result:** The GPU compute units (SMs) sit idle waiting for memory. The system is **memory-bandwidth bound**, not compute-bound.\n\n### 2. The Solution: Speculative Execution\nSpeculative decoding mimics the hardware technique of speculative execution (used in CPUs to hide branch latency). It uses a smaller, faster model (the **Draft Model**) to predict a sequence of future tokens, which are",
