    "wall_seconds": 3.6937671759806108,
    "content": "\n\nBelow is a compact but complete treatment: the failure modes you are likely hitting, and a shutdown protocol that is safe under the usual memory model (C++/Java/Go semantics). I\u2019ll assume a classic MPMC queue with a head/tail index, a fixed ring buffer, and a \"closed\" flag.\n\nLikely races that cause lost work\n- Close-then-drain race (most common): A producer sees closed == true and stops, but it had already enqueued an item that a consumer hasn\u2019t observed yet. If shutdown then destroys the queue or resets state, that item is lost.\n- Head/tail visibility race: Producer increments tail and stores the item; consumer reads tail before the item is visible (or vice versa). Without proper ordering, the consumer can skip or double-process items.\n- ABA on indices: If you use raw pointers or wrap-around indices without a sequence number, a consumer can mistake a new item for an old one (or vice versa), dropping work.\n- Double-close / close-during-drain: Multiple threads call close(); one destroys the queue while another is still draining.\n- Consumer exits too early: A consumer sees closed == true and returns, but other producers still have in-flight",
