    "power_samples": 58,
    "wall_seconds": 2.840465494984528,
    "content": "\n\nTo optimize the performance of a Large Language Model (LLM) serving system, engineers must balance **latency** (time to first token and inter-token latency) against **throughput** (tokens per second per GPU) and **cost**. Caching, batching, and speculative decoding are the three primary levers used to achieve this, each with distinct tradeoffs.\n\nHere is a summary of the tradeoffs among these three techniques:\n\n### 1. Caching (e.g., KV Cache, Prompt Caching)\nCaching stores the Key-Value (KV) tensors generated during the attention mechanism to avoid redundant computation.\n\n*   **Benefits:**\n    *   **Reduced Latency:** Significantly speeds up the prefill phase for repeated prompts (e.g., system prompts, few-shot examples) and reduces memory bandwidth pressure during decoding.\n    *   **Cost Efficiency:** Reduces the number of FLOPs required per request, lowering inference costs.\n*   **Tradeoffs:**\n    *   **Memory Overhead:** The KV cache grows linearly with sequence length and batch size. This can quickly exhaust GPU VRAM, limiting the maximum context length or batch size.\n    *   **Complexity:** Managing cache eviction",
